From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 13:01:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Implement wlr-layer-shell

zwlr_layer_shell_v1 version 5: the four layers, anchors, margins,
exclusive zones and set_exclusive_edge, keyboard interactivity none,
exclusive and on_demand, set_layer, popups through get_popup, the output a
client names or else the monitor under the pointer, monitor hotplug and
scale changes. Ported from Muffin's layer shell, with the arrangement of
wlroots' wlr_scene_layer_surface_v1_configure().

A mapped layer surface is a MetaWindow, which the layer shell alone
places. Background and bottom surfaces are desktop windows under every
normal window; top surfaces are docks above them, below a full-screen
window on their monitor and inside the window group; overlay surfaces are
docks in the layer overlay group, meta_compositor_get_layer_overlay_group(),
for the shell to stack above its windows. Exclusive zones shrink each
monitor's work area after the shell's own struts and reservations.
meta_wayland_compositor_set_layer_surfaces_hidden() puts every layer
surface and its popups away; a layer surface never inhibits shortcuts.

What a client may do is four capabilities granted per application
(MetaLayerShellCapabilities: background, panel, overlay, keyboard), set
with meta_wayland_compositor_set_layer_shell_grants(). Host clients have
background and panel, sandboxed ones nothing, the shell's own everything.
Each commit is clamped to the grants: overlay without overlay goes on top,
background without background on bottom, exclusive keyboard without
keyboard becomes on_demand (focus on a click only), an exclusive zone needs
a panel layer; a surface left with no layer is held, and shown as soon as
a grant allows it. Clamps are logged once per application and reported
once (layer-shell-clamped).

An application's surfaces covering more than 40% of an output (background
surfaces and panels in a 15% edge band excepted) are shown only while it,
or a full-screen window on that output, has the focus, or within 5 s of a
click on them; otherwise they fade to opacity 0.35 and saturation 0.7 over
180 ms and input passes through. An overlay covering more than 40% within
2 s of its process starting is refused.
meta_wayland_compositor_stop_layer_overlays() closes an application's
overlays for the session without changing its grants;
meta_wayland_compositor_get_layer_shell_surfaces() and
layer-shell-surfaces-changed report what is on screen.
